window.docContents[253].resolve({"/Tier-3___-trusted___-not-read/AArch64-semantics/Arm64.Trusted.ISA.State/#file-Arm64___Trusted___ISA___State":{"contents":"Paper › arm64-semantics/Arm64/Trusted/ISA › State.leanTier 3 · arm64-semantics\n\nTier 3 · arm64-semantics used by the symbolic proof 3 headline theorems depend on it 502 of 689 lines trusted source ↗Trusted, reviewed with the engine. The AArch64 model in arm64-semantics is Tier 3: it is reviewed in its own repository (check-trust-guards). The declarations that the headline statements use are shown for completeness.\n\nDeclarations on this page (82)structure PStatedef zerodef ofBits4structure RegFiledef emptydef readdef writestructure VRegFiledef emptydef readdef writeinductive OutputStreamdef ofFdinductive TraceEventdef ofBytesinductive ExecStatusstructure Timespecstructure Oracleinductive FsEntryinductive FdAccessModedef canReaddef canWriteinductive FdKinddef MAX_LFS_FILESIZEabbrev FsPathdef splitSlashdef hasLeadingSlashdef hasDotDotdef normalizePathdef FsPath.nonEmptyPrefixesdef joinSlashCharsdef FsPath.toPathStringstructure FdEntrystructure FsStatedef maxOpenFdsdef getEntrydef setEntrydef filesdef setFiledef fdBytesdef setFddef allocFddef fpsrMaskdef fpsrIOCdef fpsrDZCdef fpsrOFCdef fpsrUFCdef fpsrIXCdef fpsrQCstructure SigActionstructure SigAltStackstructure Statedef readVReg128def writeVReg128def readVReg64def writeVReg64def readVReg32def writeVReg32def readReg64def writeReg64def readReg32def writeReg32def readRegdef writeRegdef advancePCdef setPCdef setPStatedef setFpsrdef orFpsrdef setMemdef setStatusdef setBrkdef setPhysMemAvaildef setFsdef getSigActiondef setSigActiondef setSigAltStackdef nextOracledef exclWillFaildef stepExclOracledef appendTracedef spAligned\n\nARM64 Architectural State (Arm64.State)* Layer / Role: Arm64 Trusted Computing Base (Role::Spec, definitions only, 0 theorems).* Purpose & Key Exports: Programmer-visible AArch64 machine state (State), including condition flags (PState: n, z, c, v), 64-bit general-purpose registers (RegFile), 128-bit SIMD/FP registers (VRegFile), execution status (ExecStatus), nondeterministic oracle (Oracle, Timespec), recorded signal dispositions and alternate signal stack (SigAction, SigAltStack), and state accessors (readReg64, writeReg64, readReg32, writeReg32, readVReg128, writeVReg128, advancePC, setPC).* When to read: Read when writing or auditing State field projections in Spec.lean or checking register/flag/oracle representations; use ProofLib/State.lean for rewrite lemmas.\n\n\n\nstructure PStateL33–40 ↗#Processor condition flags (N, Z, C, V).\n\n\n\ndef zeroL44–45 ↗#All condition flags clear (NZCV = 0b0000).\n\ndef ofBits4L47–52 ↗#Construct PState from a 4-bit nzcv immediate (N=bit 3, Z=bit 2, C=bit 1, V=bit 0).\n\n\n\nstructure RegFileL56–59 ↗#General-purpose register file (X0..X30, indexed by BitVec 5).\n\n\n\ndef emptyL66–67 ↗#The all-zero general-purpose register file.\n\ndef readL69–72 ↗#Read general-purpose register idx. Note this is the raw indexed read; XZR/SP disambiguation happens in State.readReg64.\n\ndef writeL74–77 ↗#Write general-purpose register idx. Note this is the raw indexed write; XZR/SP disambiguation happens in State.writeReg64.\n\n\n\nstructure VRegFileL81–84 ↗#128-bit SIMD/FP register file (V0..V31 / Q0..Q31 / D0..D31 / S0..S31 / H0..H31 / B0..B31, indexed by BitVec 5).\n\n\n\ndef emptyL91–92 ↗#The all-zero SIMD/FP register file.\n\ndef readL94–96 ↗#Read the full 128 bits of SIMD/FP register idx.\n\ndef writeL98–100 ↗#Write the full 128 bits of SIMD/FP register idx.\n\n\n\ninductive OutputStreamL104–110 ↗#Output stream destination for byte-granular write events (stdout = fd 1, stderr = fd 2, or an arbitrary file descriptor fd).\n\n\n\ndef ofFdL114–118 ↗#Map a Linux file descriptor (1 = stdout, 2 = stderr) to its OutputStream.\n\n\n\ninductive TraceEventL122–136 ↗#Observable event in the execution trace.\n\n\n\n2 declarations not shown (lines 140–144): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesArm64.TraceEvent.stdout, Arm64.TraceEvent.stderr\n\ndef ofBytesL146–148 ↗#Convert a sequence of bytes written to stream into byte-granular TraceEvents.\n\n\n\ninductive ExecStatusL152–170 ↗#Execution status of the machine state.\n\nstructure TimespecL172–213 ↗#Per-call result of a Linux AArch64 clock_gettime syscall (struct __kernel_timespec in include/uapi/linux/time_types.h, written by put_timespec64 in kernel/time/time.c).Per-call invariants guaranteed by the Linux kernel across all modeled clock IDs 0..7 (CLOCK_REALTIME = 0, CLOCK_MONOTONIC = 1, CLOCK_PROCESS_CPUTIME_ID = 2, CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID = 3, CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW = 4, CLOCK_REALTIME_COARSE = 5, CLOCK_MONOTONIC_COARSE = 6, CLOCK_BOOTTIME = 7 in kernel/time/posix-timers.c and lib/vdso/gettimeofday.c):* Normalized nanoseconds (0 ≤ tv_nsec < 1_000_000_000, enforced by Fin 1000000000): high-resolution getters (ktime_get_real_ts64, ktime_get_ts64, ktime_get_raw_ts64 in kernel/time/timekeeping.c and vDSO do_hres in lib/vdso/gettimeofday.c) reduce nanoseconds via __iter_div_u64_rem(..., NSEC_PER_SEC, &ns); coarse getters (ktime_get_coarse_real_ts64, ktime_get_coarse_ts64 and vDSO do_coarse) and time-namespace offset additions (timens_add_monotonic, timens_add_boottime in include/linux/time_namespace.h) normalize via set_normalized_timespec64 (kernel/time/time.c), which adjusts tv_sec in both loops while (nsec >= NSEC_PER_SEC) and while (nsec < 0) so 0 ≤ tv_nsec < 1_000_000_000 even when tv_sec < 0; and CLOCK_BOOTTIME (ktime_get_boottime_ts64) and CPU clocks (posix_cpu_clock_get in kernel/time/posix-cpu-timers.c) convert s64 nanoseconds via ns_to_timespec64 (kernel/time/time.c), which likewise guarantees 0 ≤ tv_nsec < 1_000_000_000.* Seconds (sec : BitVec 64, unconstrained): CLOCK_REALTIME (0) and CLOCK_REALTIME_COARSE (5) cannot be set before the 1970 epoch (timespec64_valid in include/linux/time64.h rejects ts->tv_sec < 0, and timespec64_valid_settod is enforced by do_settimeofday64, timekeeping_inject_offset, and timekeeping_init in kernel/time/timekeeping.c, as well as do_sys_settimeofday64 in kernel/time/posix-stubs.c). In the initial time namespace (init_time_ns), all eight clocks 0..7 satisfy tv_sec ≥ 0. However, under CONFIG_TIME_NS (CLONE_NEWTIME), proc_timens_set_offset (kernel/time/namespace.c) validates a negative CLOCK_MONOTONIC offset against high-resolution ktime_get_ts64 (tp.tv_sec ≥ 0), whereas posix_get_monotonic_coarse (CLOCK_MONOTONIC_COARSE = 6) and posix_get_monotonic_raw (CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW = 4) apply that same ns->offsets.monotonic via timens_add_monotonic (kernel/time/posix-timers.c, lib/vdso/gettimeofday.c): because ktime_get_coarse_ts64 omits the sub-tick timekeeping_get_ns(&tk->tkr_mono) delta and ktime_get_raw_ts64 omits NTP slewing, timens_add_monotonic can normalize tv_sec to a negative value (e.g. -1) while keeping 0 ≤ tv_nsec < 1_000_000_000. Moreover, even for single calls, CLOCK_REALTIME can jump backward across calls (clock_settime / adjtimex). Leaving sec : BitVec 64 unconstrained with nsec : Fin 1000000000 therefore captures the exact per-call invariant guaranteed across all clocks 0..7.\n\nstructure OracleL215–251 ↗#Results of the nondeterministic system calls. Query n is the n-th nondeterministic syscall of the run, counting from zero (State.oracleIdx).Step stays deterministic. The nondeterminism comes from the initial state: specs such as subroutine_ok quantify over every entry state s0, so they hold for every s0.oracle, i.e. for every possible kernel behaviour. Indexing by the query count loses nothing: given s0, the whole history before query n is determined, so a kernel that looks at that history (including the clock ID passed to clock_gettime) is still just some function of n.* mmap n: preferred address candidate for the n-th query if it is an anonymous mmap or mremap(..., MREMAP_MAYMOVE). execMmap and execMremap sanitize the candidate (mm/mmap.c get_unmapped_area): if it is below MMAP_MIN_ADDR, not page-aligned, exceeds the 48-bit user virtual address space (2 ^ 48), or overlaps an already-mapped byte, they deterministically substitute the lowest free page-aligned gap in [MMAP_MIN_ADDR, 2 ^ 48) that fits, failing only when no free gap of the requested size exists,* mremapFail n: whether the n-th query refuses an mremap(..., MREMAP_MAYMOVE) with -ENOMEM (leaving the existing mapping intact), conservatively retained to over-approximate nondeterministic mremap refusal (e.g. RLIMIT_AS / vm.max_map_count or allocator fallback paths from mremap to mmap),* getrandom n i: byte i of the buffer filled by the n-th query if it is a getrandom,* clockGettime n: normalized struct timespec (0 ≤ nsec < 1_000_000_000) written by the n-th query if it is a successful clock_gettime, with no monotonicity relation between successive queries,* exclFails: finite schedule of spurious store-exclusive (STXR*/STLXR*/STXP/STLXP) failures copied into State.exclFails at process load time (ElfImage.loadState). Every execution has finitely many spurious failures, adversarially placed (headD false, consumed per non-faulting store-exclusive). This models eventual LL/SC progress without an exclusive monitor; a retry-less stxr is unprovable because exclFails = [true] is a valid initial state.\n\ninductive FsEntryL255–259 ↗#Inode payload in the local file system (file byte sequence or dir directory entry).\n\ninductive FdAccessModeL261–266 ↗#File-descriptor access mode (O_RDONLY, O_WRONLY, O_RDWR).\n\n\n\ndef canReadL270–273 ↗#True when the open file description permits reading (O_RDONLY or O_RDWR).\n\ndef canWriteL275–278 ↗#True when the open file description permits writing (O_WRONLY or O_RDWR).\n\n\n\ninductive FdKindL282–296 ↗#Descriptor backend tag for entries in FsState.fds:* localFile: regular file backed by FsState.files.* localDir: read-only directory descriptor (O_RDONLY, S_IFDIR | 0755) backed by FsState.files (fstat returns encodeLinuxStat .dir, read returns -EISDIR, write returns -EBADF, close returns 0, and pread64/lseek fail closed).* stdio: standard stream (stdin / stdout / stderr), stored out of band from the working-directory file namespace (FsState.entries) in FsState.stdin / State.trace.* custom (tag : Nat): extension slot for future loopback socket / pipe / peer-oracle backends sharing FsState.fds without changing any FdEntry field or invalidating file-descriptor proofs.\n\ndef MAX_LFS_FILESIZEL298–300 ↗#Linux 64-bit MAX_LFS_FILESIZE (include/linux/fs.h, LLONG_MAX = 2 ^ 63 - 1): maximum file size and byte offset (loff_t) representable in a signed 64-bit file offset.\n\nabbrev FsPathL302–303 ↗#Canonical relative path in the working-directory file-system model ([] = \".\").\n\ndef splitSlashL305–313 ↗#Split s on '/' into raw path segments using pure List Char recursion so path normalization reduces directly in the Lean kernel (rfl / decide +kernel) across module boundaries.\n\ndef hasLeadingSlashL315–317 ↗#True when path begins with '/'.\n\ndef hasDotDotL319–321 ↗#True when path contains a \"..\" path segment.\n\ndef normalizePathL323–327 ↗#Normalize a relative POSIX pathname into its canonical non-empty, non-\".\" path segments (normalizePath \"./coins\" = [\"coins\"], normalizePath \".//a/./b/\" = [\"a\", \"b\"], normalizePath \".\" = [], normalizePath \"./\" = []).\n\ndef FsPath.nonEmptyPrefixesL329–333 ↗#Non-empty path prefixes of segs in top-down order (FsPath.nonEmptyPrefixes [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"] = [[\"a\"], [\"a\", \"b\"], [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]]).\n\ndef joinSlashCharsL335–339 ↗#Join List (List Char) segments with '/' using pure List Char recursion.\n\ndef FsPath.toPathStringL341–344 ↗#Canonical String form of p : FsPath ([] ↦ \".\", [\"a\", \"b\"] ↦ \"a/b\").\n\nstructure FdEntryL346–355 ↗#Open file description entry in the process file-descriptor table (regular files, read-only directories, and standard streams).\n\nstructure FsStateL357–364 ↗#Process working-directory file-system tree (entries, keyed by canonical FsPath relative to AT_FDCWD, with [] denoting \".\"), out-of-band standard input stream bytes (stdin), and open file-descriptor table (fd ∈ [3, 1024) plus standard streams 0, 1, 2).\n\n\n\ndef maxOpenFdsL368–369 ↗#Maximum open file descriptor number + 1 (RLIMIT_NOFILE = 1024).\n\ndef getEntryL371–373 ↗#Look up canonical relative path p in fs, treating CWD ([]) as always .dir.\n\ndef setEntryL375–377 ↗#Update canonical relative path p in fs.entries.\n\ndef filesL379–381 ↗#Look up path in fs under its canonical normalizePath key.\n\ndef setFileL383–385 ↗#Update path in fs under its canonical normalizePath key ((fs.setFile \"./f\" v).files \"f\" = v by rfl).\n\ndef fdBytesL387–393 ↗#Byte sequence backing an open .localFile or .stdio descriptor (fs.stdin for .stdio, fs.files desc.path regular-file bytes for .localFile, [] otherwise).\n\ndef setFdL395–397 ↗#Update descriptor fd in fs.fds.\n\ndef allocFdL399–402 ↗#Allocate the lowest unused file descriptor fd ∈ [3, maxOpenFds).\n\n\n\ndef fpsrMaskL406–410 ↗#Modeled FPSR mask (0x0800009f): cumulative IEEE 754 exception flags IOC (bit 0), DZC (bit 1), OFC (bit 2), UFC (bit 3), IXC (bit 4), IDC (bit 7), and cumulative SIMD saturation flag QC (bit 27). All other bits ([6:5], [26:8], and AArch32-only NZCV [31:28]) are RES0.\n\ndef fpsrIOCL412–413 ↗#FPSR.IOC (bit 0): Invalid Operation cumulative exception flag.\n\ndef fpsrDZCL415–416 ↗#FPSR.DZC (bit 1): Divide by Zero cumulative exception flag.\n\ndef fpsrOFCL418–419 ↗#FPSR.OFC (bit 2): Overflow cumulative exception flag.\n\ndef fpsrUFCL421–422 ↗#FPSR.UFC (bit 3): Underflow cumulative exception flag.\n\ndef fpsrIXCL424–425 ↗#FPSR.IXC (bit 4): Inexact cumulative exception flag.\n\n1 declaration not shown (lines 427–428): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesArm64.fpsrIDC\n\ndef fpsrQCL430–431 ↗#FPSR.QC (bit 27): Cumulative SIMD saturation flag.\n\nstructure SigActionL433–444 ↗#Per-signal 32-byte kernel disposition entry (struct k_sigaction on AArch64 Linux, include/linux/signal_types.h and arch/arm64/include/uapi/asm/signal.h): handler (0 = SIG_DFL, 1 = SIG_IGN, or a recorded user-space handler address for synchronous fault signals SIGSEGV / SIGBUS), flags, restorer, and 64-bit mask. Signal dispositions are recorded and returned by rt_sigaction; asynchronous signal delivery is not modeled, and hardware faults terminate fail-closed with .Exception.\n\nstructure SigAltStackL446–458 ↗#Per-thread alternate signal stack configuration (current->sas_ss_sp, current->sas_ss_size, current->sas_ss_flags in include/linux/sched.h, include/linux/sched/signal.h:571-615, and kernel/signal.c:4458): sp (0 when disabled), size (0 when disabled, reporting SS_DISABLE = 2), and the raw stored ssFlags word (sas_ss_flags, written verbatim by do_sigaltstack in kernel/signal.c:4508; its SS_AUTODISARM = 1 << 31 bit suppresses on_sig_stack). Alternate-stack settings are recorded and returned by sigaltstack; signal delivery is not modeled.\n\nstructure StateL460–517 ↗#Complete AArch64 architectural machine state, plus the process state this model keeps for Linux syscalls and hardware nondeterminism (brkStart, brk, fs, oracle, oracleIdx, exclFails, trace, status, sigActions, sigAltStack).\n\n\n\n1 declaration not shown (lines 521–523): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesArm64.State.sym\n\ndef readVReg128L525–527 ↗#Read a 128-bit SIMD/FP register (V0..V31 / Q0..Q31).\n\ndef writeVReg128L529–531 ↗#Write a 128-bit SIMD/FP register (V0..V31 / Q0..Q31).\n\ndef readVReg64L533–535 ↗#Read the low 64 bits (D0..D31) of SIMD/FP register idx.\n\ndef writeVReg64L537–539 ↗#Write a 64-bit value (D0..D31 or 64-bit vector v.8b/v.4h/v.2s/v.1d), zero-extending to 128 bits per AArch64 architecture.\n\ndef readVReg32L541–543 ↗#Read the low 32 bits (S0..S31) of SIMD/FP register idx.\n\ndef writeVReg32L545–547 ↗#Write a 32-bit value (S0..S31), zero-extending to 128 bits per AArch64 architecture.\n\ndef readReg64L549–554 ↗#Read a 64-bit register (X0..X30, SP, or XZR).\n\ndef writeReg64L556–564 ↗#Write a 64-bit register (X0..X30, SP, or XZR). Writes to XZR are ignored.\n\ndef readReg32L566–568 ↗#Read the low 32 bits of a register (W0..W30, WSP, or WZR).\n\ndef writeReg32L570–572 ↗#Write a 32-bit value to a register (W0..W30, WSP, or WZR), zero-extending to 64 bits per AArch64 architecture.\n\ndef readRegL574–578 ↗#Read register at width w (W32 zero-extended to 64 bits, or W64).\n\ndef writeRegL580–582 ↗#Write register at width w (W32 truncates to 32 bits and zero-extends to 64 bits; W64 writes all 64 bits).\n\ndef advancePCL584–586 ↗#Advance PC by 4 bytes (s.pc + 4).\n\ndef setPCL588–590 ↗#Set PC to newPC.\n\ndef setPStateL592–594 ↗#Update PState flags.\n\ndef setFpsrL596–598 ↗#Set FPSR to fpsr.\n\ndef orFpsrL600–602 ↗#Accumulate exception or saturation flags into FPSR (s.fpsr ||| flags).\n\ndef setMemL604–606 ↗#Update memory.\n\ndef setStatusL608–610 ↗#Update execution status.\n\ndef setBrkL612–614 ↗#Update the program break (brk).\n\ndef setPhysMemAvailL616–618 ↗#Update the remaining dynamic physical Perm.rw byte budget (physMemAvail).\n\ndef setFsL620–622 ↗#Update the local file system and file-descriptor state (fs).\n\ndef getSigActionL624–626 ↗#Read the recorded kernel signal disposition for signal sig.\n\ndef setSigActionL628–630 ↗#Update the recorded kernel signal disposition for signal sig.\n\ndef setSigAltStackL632–634 ↗#Update the recorded alternate signal stack (sigAltStack).\n\ndef nextOracleL636–638 ↗#Consume one oracle query (increment oracleIdx).\n\ndef exclWillFailL640–644 ↗#Whether the next non-faulting store-exclusive (STXR*/STLXR*/STXP/STLXP) will spuriously fail (s.exclFails.headD false). Once the finite list s.exclFails is exhausted, store-exclusives succeed (false).\n\ndef stepExclOracleL646–648 ↗#Consume one store-exclusive failure decision (s.exclFails.tail).\n\ndef appendTraceL650–652 ↗#Append byte-level events to the observable execution trace (s.trace ++ evs).\n\n2 declarations not shown (lines 654–664): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesArm64.State.stdoutBytes, Arm64.State.stderrBytes\n\ndef spAlignedL673–685 ↗#Architectural stack pointer alignment predicate (CheckSPAlignment() in ARMv8-A pseudocode): whenever SP is used as the base register of a memory access, SP must be 16-byte aligned (SP[3:0] == 0b0000).Structured ASL: arm-asl-extract query CheckSPAlignment (shared_pseudocode.xml, see .agents/skills/review-arm64-asl/SKILL.md).See the ARM Architecture Reference Manual (DDI 0487):* Section D1.4.10: Program Counter and stack pointer alignment* Section C6.1.3: Use of the stack pointer* Section J1.2.305: CheckSPAlignment pseudocode\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"arm64-semantics/Arm64/Trusted/ISA/State.lean","id":"/Tier-3___-trusted___-not-read/AArch64-semantics/Arm64.Trusted.ISA.State/#file-Arm64___Trusted___ISA___State"}});